WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.103
A Poisson process counts random arrivals at a constant rate with independent increments over disjoint time intervals

00:00:07.103 --> 00:00:12.229
At rate two per hour, the expected count in one hour is two

00:00:12.229 --> 00:00:16.567
This is an average, not a promise of exactly two events.

00:00:17.867 --> 00:00:21.513
The count in one hour has a Poisson distribution

00:00:21.513 --> 00:00:29.210
Its first four probabilities correspond to zero, one, two, and three arrivals, with more possible counts beyond those shown

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:33.667
The chance of zero arrivals is e to the negative two.

00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:41.900
The waiting time to the first arrival exceeds t exactly when no arrivals occur by t

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:46.667
That gives exponential survival and mean waiting time one half hour

00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:51.867
Independent exponential interarrival times are one way to construct this Poisson process.

00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:58.957
Exponential waiting is memoryless: elapsed waiting does not change the remaining-time distribution

00:00:58.957 --> 00:01:04.265
A general renewal process only assumes identically distributed independent interarrival times

00:01:04.265 --> 00:01:13.433
Fixed half-hour intervals have the same average rate but are not memoryless and do not form a Poisson process.
